WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.018
Celebrations of the summer solstice date back to some of the earliest known

00:00:03.034 --> 00:00:07.098
civilizations. Stonehenge was estimated to have been built around 2500 BCE.

00:00:07.194 --> 00:00:10.350
And while its purpose is unknown, it is speculated to be an ancient solar

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.386
calendar, as the entrance faces the rising sun on the day of summer

00:00:13.418 --> 00:00:16.666
solstice. One of the most extravagant celebrations can be found in Sweden,

00:00:16.698 --> 00:00:20.254
where Midsummer gatherings are celebrated with decor, dancing, food and

00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:23.086
drinks. Perhaps such importance was placed on this time of year because of

00:00:23.108 --> 00:00:25.954
Sweden's high latitude on the globe, allowing what is known as the midnight

00:00:26.002 --> 00:00:28.630
sun, where daylight can be observed even in the middle of the night.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.538
The Swedish tradition of celebrating the longest day of the year is believed

00:00:31.554 --> 00:00:34.886
to have started in the 15 hundreds as a Pagan festival of fertility and lights. 

00:00:34.908 --> 00:00:38.514
These celebrations may have coincided with Vikings and sailors

00:00:38.562 --> 00:00:41.494
returning home from their voyages and mingling with the women for the first

00:00:41.532 --> 00:00:42.182
time in a while.

00:00:42.236 --> 00:00:45.674
And it became tradition to erect what is known as the Maple, which young

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.942
women were meant to dance around in a circular motion.

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:50.170
I'm sure you can figure out the symbolism there on your own.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.028
As with any ancient tradition, lore and folktales began to be associated with it.

00:00:54.032 --> 00:00:57.966
In one countryside community known as Hårga, a song called Hårgalåten was

00:00:57.988 --> 00:00:59.482
associated with the Maple dance.

00:00:59.546 --> 00:01:03.546
This song is based on a 1785 folktale about the devil disguised, as a fiddler

00:01:03.578 --> 00:01:06.670
who possessed the young to dance and dance until they collapsed and died.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.290
Basically the earliest known version of that one Yeah Yeah Yeahs song.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.750
- [Yeah Yeah Yeahs] Dance, dance til' you're dead. 

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.150
The Midsummer celebration.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.506
The Hårga, the Maple and the Hårgalåten are all real cultural phenomena, as

00:01:17.528 --> 00:01:20.246
you may have figured out if you're a fan of the Swedish metal band in

00:01:20.268 --> 00:01:22.790
Plains, who covered Hårgalåten in 1994.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.726
But the cult scene in the 2019 movie Midsommar have certain traditions that

00:01:26.748 --> 00:01:30.226
don't reflect the real life Hårga, namely human sacrifice.

00:01:30.338 --> 00:01:33.658
In the movie, the dance has evolved into sort of a competition to crown the

00:01:33.664 --> 00:01:35.462
last dancer standing as the May Queen.

00:01:35.526 --> 00:01:39.878
And in 2019, one traumatized girl finds herself thrust into the festivities

00:01:39.974 --> 00:01:43.178
to learn the entire history of the May Queen, which aspects of Swedish

00:01:43.194 --> 00:01:46.794
folklore are inspired from real life and the meaning behind all the insanity

00:01:46.842 --> 00:01:47.962
that is Midsummer.

00:01:48.026 --> 00:01:50.160
Stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.657
